# What counts as a cost, and why does the future cost less?
next: {"title":"CEA, CUA, CBA - and why cost-of-illness answers a different question entirely","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/health-technology-assessment-deciding/cea-cua-cba-and-why-cost-of-illness.cast"}

## The Price Tag Is Not the Cost
voice: female
level: basic

A committee that argues about a price tag is arguing about the wrong number — so this series takes one price apart into everything a decision must actually pay for, and then asks why a krone spent in the future counts for less than one spent today.
    text t_title "The price tag is not the cost" x 500 y 690 font_size 32
    shape tag shape rect x 500 y 460 width 460 height 120

Nusinersen for spinal muscular atrophy: around seven hundred thousand kroner a dose at list price, six doses in the first year and three a year after — roughly four million kroner in a child's first year.
    text tag_price "kr 700 000" #b5482e x 500 y 490 font_size 44
    text tag_sub "one dose of nusinersen (list price)" x 500 y 435 font_size 22
    text regimen "6 doses in year 1, then 3 a year  ≈  kr 4 mill. in year 1" x 500 y 345 font_size 24

That is the number that made the newspapers, and the number Beslutningsforum was accused of hiding behind. It is not the number the analysis uses.
    point at.ref tag_price gesture circle

    pause 0.4

The analysis needs delta C: the difference in ressursbruk — resource use — between the new technology and current care.
    text dc_line "ΔC = C₁ − C₀" x 500 y 240 font_size 36
    text dc_note "spent with the drug  −  spent without it" #8f887c x 500 y 190 font_size 22

    pause 0.5

    clear

So open the ledger, for one child, for one year.
    text led_title "One child, one year — the ΔC ledger (kr)" x 500 y 690 font_size 28

Line one, the drug itself: six doses at seven hundred thousand, four point two million kroner.
    text r1a "Drug acquisition" x 330 y 600 font_size 24
    text r1b "+ 4 200 000" #b5482e x 770 y 600 font_size 24

It does not administer itself — each dose goes into the spinal fluid, in theatre, under sedation — and the treatment obliges motor testing, blood work and clinic visits.
    text r2a "Injection: theatre, sedation, imaging" x 330 y 530 font_size 22
    text r2b "+ 150 000" #b5482e x 770 y 530 font_size 24
    text r3a "Monitoring: motor tests, labs, visits" x 330 y 460 font_size 22
    text r3b "+ 80 000" #b5482e x 770 y 460 font_size 24

    pause 0.3

Then a line that points the other way.
    highlight r4a r4b effect glow

Ventilator hours and intensive care this child no longer needs are subtracted, because delta C is a difference, not a bill — a treatment can buy back some of its own price.
    text r4a "Ventilation and ICU avoided" x 330 y 390 font_size 22
    text r4b "− 300 000" #2f6b8f x 770 y 390 font_size 24

    pause 0.3

And two lines nobody invoices: the parents' hours of care, and the health care a longer life will use for things entirely unrelated to this disease.
    text r5a "Informal care by the family" x 330 y 320 font_size 22
    text r5b "+ ?" #8a5fa8 x 770 y 320 font_size 24
    text r6a "Future unrelated health care" x 330 y 250 font_size 22
    text r6b "+ ?" #8a5fa8 x 770 y 250 font_size 24

Four point one million kroner in year one — plus two question marks we never priced.
    path rule #8f887c points [[170,210],[830,210]]
    text led_total "ΔC ≈ 4 130 000  +  two question marks" x 500 y 160 font_size 28

The headline price was one line of this page.
    mark mark_total target led_total kind box
    delivery grave

    pause 0.5

    clear

Four questions follow. Where does a unit cost even come from — micro-costing every minute and syringe, or the DRG average the funding system already pays per hospital stay?
    text q_title "Four questions in this series" x 500 y 690 font_size 30
    text q1 "1.  Where do unit costs come from?" x 500 y 570 font_size 26

When a parent leaves work, does that lost production belong in delta C — and does human capital or friction cost describe what society actually loses?
    text q2 "2.  Should lost production count?" x 500 y 480 font_size 26

Norway discounts costs and health effects at four percent a year, which halves a QALY arriving eighteen years from now — and a child's benefits arrive decades away.
    text q3 "3.  Why does the future cost less?" x 500 y 390 font_size 26

And which costs never get booked at all: informal care, unrelated future treatment, and the assessment itself, which is also not free.
    text q4 "4.  Which costs never get booked?" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 300 font_size 26

A price is one line; delta C is the whole page — and the page is dated.
    text closing "A price is one line. ΔC is the whole page." x 500 y 170 font_size 26
    mark mark_closing target closing kind box

## Micro-Costing or DRG Average?
voice: female
level: basic
domain: {"x":[0,40],"y":[0,1]}

    text t_title "One intrathecal administration, built from the bottom" x 500 y 690 font_size 30 hidden true
    text r1_item "Neurologist, 45 min" x 340 y 600 font_size 26 hidden true
    text r1_cost "kr 1,100" x 710 y 600 font_size 26 hidden true
    text r2_item "Two nurses, 60 min" x 340 y 545 font_size 26 hidden true
    text r2_cost "kr 900" x 710 y 545 font_size 26 hidden true
    text r3_item "Procedure room, 60 min" x 340 y 490 font_size 26 hidden true
    text r3_cost "kr 2,400" x 710 y 490 font_size 26 hidden true
    text r4_item "LP tray, drapes, ultrasound" x 340 y 435 font_size 26 hidden true
    text r4_cost "kr 700" x 710 y 435 font_size 26 hidden true
    text r5_item "Observation, 4 hours" x 340 y 380 font_size 26 hidden true
    text r5_cost "kr 1,400" x 710 y 380 font_size 26 hidden true
    path rule #8f887c points [[190,345],[800,345]] hidden true
    text total_item "Micro-costed total" #2f6b8f x 340 y 300 font_size 30 hidden true
    text total_cost "kr 6,500" #2f6b8f x 710 y 300 font_size 34 hidden true
    shape box_micro #2f6b8f shape rect x 300 y 505 width 300 height 170 hidden true
    text micro_title "Micro-costed" #2f6b8f x 300 y 552 font_size 26 hidden true
    text micro_num "kr 6,500" #2f6b8f x 300 y 492 font_size 38 hidden true
    shape box_drg #b5482e shape rect x 700 y 505 width 300 height 170 hidden true
    text drg_title "DRG tariff (ISF)" #b5482e x 700 y 552 font_size 26 hidden true
    text drg_num "kr 11,300" #b5482e x 700 y 492 font_size 38 hidden true
    arrow gap_arrow box_micro -> box_drg #8f887c hidden true
    label gap_label "kr 4,800 apart" below #8f887c attach_to gap_arrow hidden true

Now that we have seen that a price tag is not a cost, someone has to put a number on one procedure — and the two available routes can disagree by a factor of two.
    draw t_title

The bottom-up route, micro-costing, counts ingredients: a neurologist for the forty-five minute lumbar puncture, two nurses for the hour.
    draw r1_item r1_cost r2_item r2_cost

An hour of staffed, sterile procedure room is the biggest line, two thousand four hundred; the tray, drapes and ultrasound gel add seven hundred.
    draw r3_item r3_cost r4_item r4_cost

Then four hours of observation, because a child who has had nusinersen injected into the spine does not go straight home.
    draw r5_item r5_cost

Six thousand five hundred kroner to deliver one dose — the price of the vial itself is a separate, and confidential, conversation.
    draw rule total_item total_cost

    pause 0.4

Every row is a quantity times a unit price, so a reviewer can argue with any single line, and you can re-cost the whole thing when the protocol changes.
    highlight total_cost effect glow

The top-down route skips all of that.
    clear

You take the DRG the case is coded to, multiply its weight by the national unit price set each year in the state budget, and read off eleven thousand three hundred kroner.
    draw box_drg drg_title drg_num

Same patient, same hour, nearly five thousand kroner apart.
    draw box_micro micro_title micro_num gap_arrow gap_label

    pause 0.4

The gap is not sloppiness — the DRG system was built at Yale in the nineteen-seventies to pay hospitals per case, so the tariff carries ward overhead and a share of everything else the hospital does.
    text note1 "A DRG weight × a national unit price" x 500 y 320 font_size 26
    text note2 "Built to finance hospitals, not to describe a patient" x 500 y 265 font_size 26

Which makes it a negotiated reimbursement price: an opinion about financing, not a measurement of what this patient consumed.
    text note3 "= a reimbursement price, not resource use" #b5482e x 500 y 200 font_size 30

So when is the shelf number good enough?
    clear

    axes ax3 x_label "Cost of one administration (kr 1,000s)" y_label Patients
    curve baseline #8f887c expr "0" style.opacity 0.15

Here is what one administration really costs across everyone coded into that same DRG — the numbers are illustrative, the shape is not.
    curve cost_curve expr 0.166*x*x*exp(-x/3.25)

Most sit near the hump: an uncomplicated puncture in a cooperative patient, close to our six and a half thousand.
    dot pt_typical #2f6b8f at.x 6.5 at.y 0.95
    label lbl_typical "Straightforward puncture ≈ kr 6,500" above-right #2f6b8f attach_to pt_typical

The tariff sits further right, pulled there by overhead and by the difficult cases it also has to cover.
    dot pt_avg #b5482e at.x 11.3 at.y 0.65 guides true
    label lbl_avg "DRG rate: kr 11,300" right #b5482e attach_to pt_avg

And out in the tail is our SMA child with scoliosis and spinal rods: sedation, an anaesthetist, image guidance — around twenty-six thousand every dose.
    region tail_region x_from 18 x_to 40 between ["cost_curve","baseline"] style.fill #c96567 style.opacity 0.3
    label lbl_tail "Unusual patients live here" below-right #8f887c attach_to tail_region
    dot pt_sma #8a5fa8 at.x 26 at.y 0.04
    text lbl_sma "Our SMA child ≈ kr 26,000" #8a5fa8 at.data [27,0.2] font_size 24

    pause 0.4

For a hospital's accounts the average is fine; for this child the tariff understates real resource use by more than half — and understates it in the direction that flatters the new technology.
    mark mark_sma target lbl_sma kind box
    delivery grave

The average is a fair description of nobody in particular, so micro-cost the few items your decision turns on, take the tariff for the rest, and say which you took where.

## Does Lost Production Count?
voice: female
level: basic
domain: {"x":[0,20],"y":[0,700]}
sources:
    - id: koopmanschap1995
      title: The friction cost method for measuring indirect costs of disease
      authors: Koopmanschap et al.
      year: 1995
      finding: Production loss lasts only until the job is refilled

    axes ax x_label "Years after leaving work" y_label "Lost output (1000 kr per year)" hidden true
    curve zero_line dashed #8f887c expr "0" style.opacity 0.35 hidden true
    curve hc_curve #b5482e expr 650*exp(-0.0392*x) hidden true
    label label_hc "Her yearly output, discounted" above #b5482e attach_to hc_curve hidden true
    region hc_region x_from 0 x_to 20 between ["hc_curve","zero_line"] style.fill #c96567 style.opacity 0.22 hidden true
    label label_hc_total "Human capital ≈ 8.8 mill. kr" below-right #b5482e attach_to hc_region hidden true
    arrow friction_line dashed #2f6b8f from.x 0.25 from.y 0 to.x 0.25 to.y 690 hidden true
    region friction_region x_from 0 x_to 0.25 between ["hc_curve","zero_line"] style.fill #2f6b8f style.opacity 0.55 hidden true
    label label_friction "Friction period: 3 months" above-right #2f6b8f attach_to friction_line hidden true
    label label_friction_total "Friction cost ≈ 160 000 kr" right #2f6b8f attach_to friction_region cites ["koopmanschap1995"] hidden true
    person n_child "Child with SMA" hidden true
    person n_parent "Parent" hidden true
    box n_loss "Production loss" hidden true
    edge e1 n_child -> n_parent hidden true
    label label_e1 "Care at home" above attach_to e1 hidden true
    edge e2 n_parent -> n_loss hidden true
    label label_e2 "Leaves or cuts paid work" above attach_to e2 hidden true

Now that we have seen a procedure's price built up bottom-up or read off the DRG average, we turn to a cost that appears in no hospital ledger at all: the work the patient stops doing.
    draw ax zero_line

Our patient is a 45-year-old machine operator who leaves the labour force after a stroke, twenty years before retirement, on 650 000 kroner a year — this line is the output she no longer produces, discounted at four per cent.
    draw hc_curve label_hc

The human capital approach counts the whole shaded block: every remaining working year, about 8.8 million kroner in present value.
    draw hc_region label_hc_total

    pause 0.4

On this accounting one stroke costs society an entire career, which quietly assumes her chair stays empty until 2045.
    highlight hc_region effect glow

The friction cost approach asks a narrower question: how long is the chair actually empty?
    draw friction_line label_friction

With people looking for work, the post is advertised, filled and trained in roughly three months — so the loss is only this sliver at the left edge.
    draw friction_region

    camera center.x 6 center.y 400 zoom 1.7

Close up, the sliver has a value: about 160 000 kroner, recruitment and training included.
    draw label_friction_total

Same patient, same stroke: 8.8 million, or 160 000 — a factor of fifty, settled by a modelling convention rather than by anything that happened to her.
    camera reset true

    pause 0.4

The sliver's logic comes from Rotterdam in the mid-nineties, where Koopmanschap and colleagues argued that if unemployed workers exist, production returns once the vacancy is filled — so society loses the transition, not the career.
    highlight friction_region effect pulse

Human capital assumes nobody ever takes her place; friction assumes someone always can — neither is true everywhere, so the method is chosen, not derived.
    point at.ref hc_region gesture underline

And human capital carries an uncomfortable corollary: it makes the identical stroke cheap in a pensioner and expensive in a lawyer.
    highlight label_hc_total effect glow

Norwegian guidance therefore keeps production changes out of the base-case ICER and asks for them separately, so a decision never hinges on the patient's payslip.
    clear

Now put our running case in the same frame: an infant with spinal muscular atrophy, who earns nothing and will not for two decades.
    draw n_child

So the patient's own production loss is zero under either method — yet the loss is real, and it sits one household member away.
    draw e1 label_e1 n_parent

A parent drops to part time, or leaves work entirely, to provide care at home; that is where the production loss in the SMA case actually lands.
    draw e2 label_e2 n_loss

Count that box and nusinersen looks better, because a treated child means a parent back at work; leave it out and the same drug looks worse — nothing about the child changed.
    mark mark_loss target n_loss kind box

Lost production is not a measurement but an assumption about what society is short of, workers or time: so state the method, state whose earnings you counted, and show the ICER both ways.
    delivery grave

## The Costs Nobody Books
voice: female
level: basic
use: tornado_diagram
with:
    title: Which omission moves the ratio?
    x_label: ICER (million kr per QALY)
    base: 3.5
    show_values: true
    bars:
      - {label: Informal care, low: 2.75, high: 3.5}
      - {label: Unrelated future costs, low: 3.5, high: 3.9}
      - {label: Production loss, low: 3.2, high: 3.5}
      - {label: Cost of the assessment, low: 3.49, high: 3.51}

Now that we have seen what four percent a year does to a child's future health, one question is left: which costs never got booked at all.
    draw axes base_line

Our nusinersen base case sits on this line — an illustrative three and a half million kroner per QALY, counting only what the guideline asks us to count.
    highlight base_line effect glow

    pause 0.3

Parents of an untreated child with spinal muscular atrophy hand over hours that no invoice records; book that informal care and the comparator arm gets dearer, so DeltaC shrinks and the ratio falls toward two and three quarter million.
    draw bar_label_0 bar_0

    pause 0.4

The treated child also lives longer, and those added years contain hip replacements, pneumonias, everything unrelated to the disease; book those unrelated future medical costs and the ratio climbs the other way.
    draw bar_label_1 bar_1

The two biggest omissions point in opposite directions, because one lands in the comparator arm and the other in the treated one.
    highlight bar_0 bar_1 effect glow delivery grave

    pause 0.3

Lost production, the argument of part three, belongs here to the parents rather than the patient, and shifts the ratio less than their care hours do.
    draw bar_label_2 bar_2

And the cost of the assessment itself — the analysts, the submissions, the Beslutningsforum agenda item.
    draw bar_label_3 bar_3

It is the thin one.
    camera center.ref bar_3 zoom 2.5 delivery soft

Worth knowing once for the system's own accounts, and worth almost nothing inside this ratio.
    camera reset true

So the rule is not count everything: an omission moves the ratio only when it falls unevenly on the two arms.
    focus bar_0 bar_1

A cost both arms carry alike — the same GP visits, the same travel — cancels inside DeltaC whether you write it down or not.
    highlight base_line effect pulse

For the boring comparator — a hip replacement at a few hundred thousand kroner per QALY — that same informal-care column is a large share of a small DeltaC, so leaving it out can reorder the whole list.
    point at.ref bar_0 gesture underline

    pause 0.4

Strip out what cannot move it, and two columns are left.
    clear keep ["axes","base_line","bar_0","bar_label_0","bar_1","bar_label_1"]

Informal care is the larger of them, and the one a Norwegian base case most often leaves off the sheet — which is exactly why it belongs in its own visible row.
    mark mark_informal target bar_label_0 kind box

Across all five parts: a cost is the ressursbruk — the resource use — that something else could have had, micro-costed or DRG-averaged, discounted at four percent, and booked even when nobody sends an invoice; what decides the ratio is never the price tag, but the difference between two arms.
    draw title

